      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Abstract Marcuse\u2019s espousal of the relative autonomy of aesthetic form in his late works has been interpreted as evidence of the transcendental detachment of his aesthetic theory from social praxis. Conversely, positive appraisals attribute value to Marcuse\u2019s purported utopianism, grounded in his occasional borrowings from Kant\u2019s formalist aesthetics. This paper reconstructs the evolution of Marcuse\u2019s early period aesthetic theory, aiming to elaborate his praxis-oriented perspective on art and society that surpasses Kant\u2019s transcendental exposition, a framework implied in both critical and positive appraisals. By discussing Marcuse\u2019s approach to the \u201cart\u2013life problem\u201d in his first work, The German Artist-Novel, I establish a backdrop against which to assess Marcuse\u2019s aesthetic writings. In this early work, Marcuse employed a neo-Kantian methodology to demonstrate how the art form of the artist-novel genre reflects the problematic character of modern life. While surpassing Kant\u2019s reduction of aesthetics to reflective judgment by considering artworks as having exemplary validity in explaining social reality, this neo-Kantian method is still transcendental and, for this reason, inherently lacks a systematic theory of the production process in modern society. I will show that Marcuse\u2019s engagement with Hegel in Hegel\u2019s Ontology and the Theory of Historicity marks a departure from transcendental inquiry into art forms and lays the groundwork for his \u201caesthetic ontology,\u201d which integrates the ontology of labour. This shift from neo-Kantianism is evident in Marcuse\u2019s interpretation of Hegel, particularly in how Hegel recasts Kant\u2019s theory of imagination in terms of the historicity of the \u201coriginal synthetic unity of thought and being.\u201d As I demonstrate, Marcuse situates different aspects of this ontological unity \u2013 \u201csemblance,\u201d \u201cappearance,\u201d and \u201cbeautiful shining-forth\u201d \u2013 within the human productive activity in the material context of life. The article further demonstrates how Marcuse\u2019s later works on culture incorporate the framework of Hegel\u2019s Ontology into a Marxian historical critique of bourgeois art, revealing the beautiful \u201cshining-forth\u201d as a non-alienated labour of a \u201cfree people.\u201d This interpretation offers a renewed perspective on aesthetic ontology, contesting the mischaracterisation of Marcuse\u2019s aesthetic theory as formalistic, subjectivist, and overly utopian.",
